# NdFeB SPS campaign

A persistent computational investigation and staff-operated evidence handoff for
NdFeB spark-plasma sintering (SPS). The material question is whether a
lower-temperature condition can maintain density while limiting grain growth
relative to a credible reference. The eventual objective is a reproducible,
useful material/process capability—not merely an agent transcript.

The system preserves executable models, predictions made before execution,
specimen history, operator review, raw deliveries and the evidence actually
available to each next decision. Scientific choices are open: the investigator
may change its model, retain it, request another measurement or stop. Provenance
and execution constraints are enforced; a winning intervention is not scripted.

**Scope:** source-specific density models, conditional synthetic SPS/density/
microstructure responses, isolated executable investigation, native Dynamical
simulation and durable operator handoff. This is not an RPI-calibrated twin, a
live instrument driver, magnetic-grade qualification, demonstrated economic
improvement or proof of transferable scientific learning. No live scientific
result is claimed by this setup guide.

## Start here

For a new session in this existing local workspace, read `START_HERE.md`, then
[the current runtime and continuation target](docs/LINUX_RUNTIME.md),
[the evaluation contract](docs/EVALUATION.md) and
[the first-return procedure](docs/RPI_DATA_RETURN.md). These identify the current
Astra investigator and accepted RPI plan; older run paths below preserve history.

`main` contains the verified source, locks, tests and operating documentation.
Start new implementation work on a task branch from `main`. Private campaign
evidence, correspondence and installed runtimes remain locally available but
ignored by Git. A code checkout alone does not reproduce the live investigator's
state; resume its recorded directory and runtime rather than initializing another
physical campaign. This repository currently has no configured remote.

- [Distribution-aware simulation and conservative fitted models](docs/FITTED_MODELS.md)
- [Physical density and microscopy reductions](docs/RPI_REDUCTIONS.md)
- [Repository map, component ownership and file placement](docs/REPOSITORY_MAP.md)
- [Campaign question and evidence boundaries](docs/CAMPAIGN.md)
- [Source calibration, uncertainty and model applicability](docs/CALIBRATION.md)
- [Delayed operator workflow and physical import](docs/OPERATOR.md)
- [Native Dynamical verification and independent admission](docs/DYNAMICAL.md)
- [Explicit infrastructure reuse](docs/REUSE.md)
- [Pi investigator, exact model routes and restart](agents/README.md)
- [Transport and accounting contract](agents/TRANSPORT.md)
- [Source provenance and rights](sources/README.md)

The earlier HPMS campaign is unchanged and is not a runtime dependency. Its
response equations, observations and authority were not relabeled as SPS.

The September 13 scientific-coverage extension adds an opt-in constructed
microstructure world (`init-simulation --microstructure`) and retrievable model
tools. It keeps grain distributions, coarse tails, section porosity and unresolved
chemistry distinct. Historical scalar worlds remain unchanged. The private v10
report and RPI request are in `artifacts/runs/scientific-coverage-v10/`.
Use the runtime recorded for the specific campaign; do not reinstall over a live
or paused investigation. The first v10 native return is verified. The explicit
model-route migration and live Astra continuation are recorded in
`artifacts/runs/astra-continuation-v11/`: native Pi, direct OpenAI Responses,
high reasoning and unbounded model calls, with the same scientific environment,
history, source packet and specimen budget.

## Clean-checkout setup

Requirements: Python 3.11+, `uv`, Node >=22.19.0/npm and a running Docker engine. The
reviewed numerical image is Linux arm64; other architectures require a separate
verified build. From the repository root:

```sh
uv sync --frozen --no-editable
npm --prefix agents ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund
```

For a disposable local demonstration, create an external run directory. For a
real delayed investigation, use a durable private directory and retain it with
the runtime record. This transferred workspace explicitly uses Git-ignored
`artifacts/` inside the project; guards verify the exclusion and reject tracked
private files. An external directory remains supported.

```sh
NDFEB_RUN_ROOT="$(mktemp -d)"
docker build --file agents/Dockerfile.science \
  --tag ndfeb-sps-science:local \
  --iidfile "$NDFEB_RUN_ROOT/science-image.iid" agents
export NDFEB_SCIENCE_IMAGE="$(cat "$NDFEB_RUN_ROOT/science-image.iid")"
export NDFEB_PYTHON="$PWD/.venv/bin/python"
```

The image override must be the immutable `sha256:...` identity, not the tag.
The executor does not pull missing images or silently choose a fallback. Preserve
this identity on restart. Numerical jobs receive released scientific inputs,
not the campaign database, owner scenario or host credentials.

The non-editable install avoids relying on a `.pth` pointer during long operator
delays. In the inspected macOS Python 3.13 environment, a hidden filesystem flag
on that pointer caused editable imports to disappear. For development, explicit
`PYTHONPATH=src` selects current source; rebuild the installed package after any
reviewed change. Do not change active investigator runtime fingerprints. For an
authorized platform or harness repair, use the stopped-copy migration described
in [the Linux runtime guide](docs/LINUX_RUNTIME.md); preserve the predecessor.

Run local checks after the image is available:

```sh
uv run --frozen --no-editable pytest
uv run --frozen --no-editable ruff check src tests
npm --prefix agents test
uv run --frozen --no-editable ndfeb-sps --help
```

Tests include synthetic fixtures and mocked provider responses. They establish
their stated software behavior, not autonomous scientific competence. The
separate intentional upstream requested/applied failure in the Dynamical
integration is documented rather than mislabeled as a passing check.

## Initialize an open investigation

```sh
NDFEB_STATE="$NDFEB_RUN_ROOT/campaign"
NDFEB_INVESTIGATOR="$NDFEB_RUN_ROOT/investigator"
uv run --frozen --no-editable ndfeb-sps init-simulation \
  --state "$NDFEB_STATE" --run-dir "$NDFEB_INVESTIGATOR" \
  --campaign-id sps-simulation-1 --max-specimens 36
```

This tests/adopts an explicitly owner-supplied source-only prior and stores a
public source packet with a human-proposed starting panel. It submits no batch,
selects no hidden world, executes no manufacture and makes no inference request.
The investigator can assess the proposed panel; its subsequent policy is not
specified by initialization. The 36-specimen envelope is an example computational
resource limit, not physical powder inventory or a scientific stopping rule.

After confirming inference authority and the exact provider route, supply the
key through the calling process environment, never a repository file or CLI
argument. For example:

```sh
node agents/launch.mjs "$NDFEB_STATE" "$NDFEB_INVESTIGATOR" \
  z-ai/glm-5.3-flash 100
```

The exact alternative route is `deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash`; use a separately
initialized state and investigator directory for an independent model comparison.
The final argument is a cumulative wire-request envelope, including compaction
and interrupted attempts. It is not a required number of scientific actions.
Known costs and unresolved charges remain distinct. See the agent guide for
transport verification and resource accounting.

## Close a synthetic operator loop

Native execution first requires the isolated runtime, verification and owner
admission described in [DYNAMICAL.md](docs/DYNAMICAL.md). A clean checkout contains
a pending proposal, not portable laboratory authority. A pending provider stays
HOLD; do not remove the gate to make a demonstration run.

The owner selects and preserves a declared scenario JSON and seed outside the
investigator context. `ndfeb_sps.models.synthetic_worlds()` supplies density
tradeoff, plateau and no-microstructure-benefit alternatives; these are
constructed stress tests, not measurements or calibrated RPI noise. Keep the
same world/seed within a continuing investigation.

Command support and realized-response support are distinct. For example, an
800°C request can produce a simulated readback above the source-supported
temperature range. The simulator then fails closed instead of clamping that
readback or inventing an extrapolated material result. Record the operational
limitation and let the investigator choose an interior request or seek a reviewed
capability extension; do not silently change the world mid-investigation.

When the investigator has committed a request, inspect its actual request ID
with `status`, then fulfill that request:

```sh
uv run --frozen --no-editable ndfeb-sps status --state "$NDFEB_STATE"
uv run --frozen --no-editable ndfeb-sps fulfill-simulation \
  --state "$NDFEB_STATE" --request-id "$NDFEB_REQUEST_ID" \
  --runtime "$NDFEB_NATIVE_RUNTIME" --artifacts "$NDFEB_RUN_ROOT/owner-artifacts" \
  --world-config "$NDFEB_WORLD_CONFIG" --seed 731
```

Set the three named input variables to the existing request, reviewed runtime
and owner-private scenario file. The helper records simulation-only operator
review, executes the admitted native provider, validates/replays its trace,
preserves raw synthetic records and **receives but does not release** them.
Unsupported/native failures remain operational failures, not failed material
specimens. Repeated fulfillment of an already received request does not
manufacture it again.

Review the returned delivery ID and original records before release:

```sh
uv run --frozen --no-editable ndfeb-sps verify --state "$NDFEB_STATE"
uv run --frozen --no-editable ndfeb-sps release --state "$NDFEB_STATE" \
  --delivery-id "$NDFEB_DELIVERY_ID" --reviewer simulation-owner
node agents/launch.mjs "$NDFEB_STATE" "$NDFEB_INVESTIGATOR" \
  z-ai/glm-5.3-flash 100
```

The last command resumes the same investigation after the operator delay. The
agent decides whether evidence warrants another model, justified retention,
another request or stopping. A follow-up request preserves its own fresh
pre-execution forecast. Evaluate a released request with
`ndfeb-sps evaluate --state ... --request-id ...`; keep requested-condition
prediction scores separate from analyses using later delivered settings.

For actual laboratory returns, use the [operator guide](docs/OPERATOR.md), not
`fulfill-simulation`. Physical execution remains HOLD in this software; the
importer records externally executed observations with explicit provenance and
owner review. A successful computational loop does not establish a physical
material advantage, dependable scientific learning or transfer. Those remain
separate prospective tests.

### Optional same-evidence forecast baselines

Before fulfilling a committed request, the owner can call
`ndfeb_sps.comparators.freeze_comparators(campaign, request_id, output_directory)`.
It runs the source-only prior and a declared local-actual-temperature comparator
twice each in Docker, saving executable bytes, original evidence cutoff and
predictions outside Git. Later corrections cannot rewrite that cutoff. A first
save after target delivery is labeled retrospective. These baselines predict the
agent's requested actions; they do not provide a matched experiment-selection or
training comparison. They are not released to the investigator during a run.

